# Rendering Iterables · Components · Reflex Docs
created: undefined, last updated: 2026-07-13, [Read more](https://reflex.dev/docs/components/rendering-iterables/)

Recall again from the basics that we cannot use Python `for` loops when referencing state vars in Reflex.
Instead, use the `rx.foreach` component to render components from a collection of data.
...
The first argument of the `rx.foreach` function is the state var that you want to iterate through.
The second argument is a function that takes in an item from the data and returns a component.
...
```
class IterIndexState(rx.State):
color: list[str] = [
"red",
"green",
"blue",
]
def create_button(color: rx.Var[str], index: int):
return rx.box(
rx.button(f"{index + 1}. {color}"),
padding_y="0.5em",
)
def enumerate_foreach():
return rx.vstack(
rx.foreach(IterIndexState.color, create_button),
)
```
...
```
def enumerate_foreach():
return rx.vstack(
rx.foreach(
IterIndexState.color, lambda color, index: create_button(color, index)
),
)
```
...
`rx.foreach` can be used with nested state vars.
Here we use nested `foreach` components to render the nested state vars.

# Access states as plain Python value for serialization
created: 2025-05-26, last updated: 2026-03-20, [Read more](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79639054/access-states-as-plain-python-value-for-serialization)

class StateA(rx.State):
value: str = "Hello"
class StateB(rx.State):
value: str = "World"
class ConfirmButton(rx.State):
did_confirm: bool = False
@rx.event
def confirm(self):
self.did_confirm = True
import json
with open("x.json", mode='w') as h:
h.write(json.dumps({
"word1": StateA.value,
"word2": StateB.value,
}))
```
